        "original_id": "0c9d3338-63e3-58ab-a8d5-e6e8c0762c8e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Isaak Dunayevsky, Grigori Aleksandrov, Sergey Agranyan, Mark Lisyansky\n- Roles: film composer, conductor, film director, lyricists\n- Works: Moya Moskva\n- Topics: Soviet film, operetta, film comedies\n\nFacts:\n- Isaak Osipovich Dunayevsky was born on 30 January 1900 and died on 25 July 1955.\n- Grigori Vasilyevich Aleksandrov was born on 23 January 1903 and died on 16 December 1983.\n- In 1941, Isaak Dunayevsky composed the music for Moya Moskva, and Sergey Agranyan and Mark Lisyansky wrote its lyrics.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky was a Soviet film composer and conductor of the 1930s and 1940s who achieved major success in operetta and film comedy music.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky frequently worked with the Soviet film director Grigori Aleksandrov.\n- In 1947, Grigori Aleksandrov was named a People's Artist of the USSR.\n- In 1973, Grigori Aleksandrov was named a Hero of Socialist Labor.",
